[{"url":"/slides-demo","title":"Slides Demo","content":"A demonstration of the slides feature with images, code, lists, and more This is the title slide — content before the first heading. It tests that pre-heading content renders as its own slide."},{"url":"/slides-demo","title":"Slides Demo","section":"The Seven Pillars","sectionId":"the-seven-pillars","content":"The Tariqa Karkariya is built upon **seven foundational practices**, each serving a distinct role in the spiritual journey of the *murid*. > \"Whoever does not have a wird has no warid.\"\n> — Sufi maxim"},{"url":"/slides-demo","title":"Slides Demo","section":"Wird — The Daily Litany","sectionId":"wird--the-daily-litany","content":"The first obligation of the murid and the foundation upon which all other practices rest. The Wird consists of: **Quranic verses** — selected by the Shaykh **Prayers upon the Prophet** — salawat **Divine names** — specific adhkar **Supplication** — personal du'a It is recited at *specific times* — typically after Fajr and after Maghrib."},{"url":"/slides-demo","title":"Slides Demo","section":"Hadra — The Gathering","sectionId":"hadra--the-gathering","content":"The collective spiritual gathering combining dhikr, poetry, and presence. Recitation of litanies Qasidas (spiritual poetry) Standing dhikr with movement Collective supplication"},{"url":"/slides-demo","title":"Slides Demo","section":"Code & Technical Content","sectionId":"code--technical-content","content":"Slides can include code blocks: And inline code like `bismillah` renders normally too."},{"url":"/slides-demo","title":"Slides Demo","section":"Tables in Slides","sectionId":"tables-in-slides","content":"Pillar Domain Practice Wird Tongue Daily litany Hadra Community Collective dhikr Ism al-Mufrad Heart Personal name Muraqqa' Mind Self-observation Siyaha Body Spiritual travel Khalwa Spirit Retreat Sirr Essence The secret"},{"url":"/slides-demo","title":"Slides Demo","section":"Math & Formulas","sectionId":"math--formulas","content":"The relationship between the pillars follows a pattern: Where each   represents the degree of realization in each pillar."},{"url":"/slides-demo","title":"Slides Demo","section":"The Three Stages","sectionId":"the-three-stages","content":"**Shari'a** (Law) Outward compliance The body's station **Tariqa** (Path) Inward refinement The heart's station **Haqiqa** (Reality) Direct witnessing The spirit's station > The three are not sequential but simultaneous — like the shell, the kernel, and the oil of a nut."},{"url":"/slides-demo","title":"Slides Demo","section":"Summary","sectionId":"summary","content":"Together, these seven pillars create a **complete framework** for spiritual development. Each practice addresses a different dimension: Dimension Pillars Tongue Wird, Hadra Heart Ism al-Mufrad Mind Muraqqa' Body Siyaha, Khalwa Spirit Sirr Visit the dictionary to explore each term in depth. *End of presentation*"},{"url":"/articles","title":"Articles","content":"Published AFFiNE pages in Articles."},{"url":"/articles","title":"Articles","section":"Articles","sectionId":"articles","content":"On Faqr: The Secret of the Path On the Wird: Why It Cannot Be Skipped Style Test What is the Tariqa?"},{"url":"/articles/on-faqr","title":"On Faqr: The Secret of the Path","content":"An essay-style teaching from the Shaykh on spiritual poverty as the destination of the Tariqa. *From a public lecture, Fes. Translated from Arabic.* People come to the Tariqa for different reasons. Some come because they are suffering and seek relief. Some come because they are curious about the spiritual life. Some come because they heard something beautiful and want more of it. Some come because someone they love is in the path and they followed out of love. All of these are fine reasons. God "},{"url":"/articles/on-faqr","title":"On Faqr: The Secret of the Path","section":"Why this is not depressing","sectionId":"why-this-is-not-depressing","content":"When people hear \"you are nothing,\" they become sad. But the one who has tasted faqr does not weep over it — he rejoices in it. Because the one who is nothing before God becomes filled with God. The empty cup receives. The full cup — full of itself, full of its own importance — has no room. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"How wonderful is the affair of the believer! All of it is good.\" This is the experience of the faqir: even difficulty becomes a gift, because even difficulty is the action of the Beloved."},{"url":"/articles/on-faqr","title":"On Faqr: The Secret of the Path","section":"Faqr and the seven pillars","sectionId":"faqr-and-the-seven-pillars","content":"Each of the seven pillars — Wird, Hadra, Muraqqa', Ism al-Mufrad, Siyaha, Khalwa, Sirr — serves faqr. Each one, in its own way, removes something of the ego's pretension. The wird humbles the morning. The hadra shows you that you are not alone. The muraqaba reveals the ego's movements. The Ism al-Mufrad replaces the ego's voice with the divine name. The siyaha loosens the ego's attachment to place. The khalwa strips the ego of social support. And the sirr — the innermost — is where the ego final"},{"url":"/articles/on-the-wird","title":"On the Wird: Why It Cannot Be Skipped","content":"The Shaykh's teaching on the centrality and non-negotiability of the daily wird. *From a teaching circle, translated from Arabic.* A student came to the Shaykh and said: \"My days are very full. I am working, raising children, there is much pressure. Can I do the wird every other day?\" The Shaykh was quiet for a moment. Then he said: \"Do you eat every other day?\" \"No, Shaykh.\" \"Do you sleep every other day?\" \"No.\" \"Then why should the wird be different? The body needs food and rest every day. The"},{"url":"/articles/on-the-wird","title":"On the Wird: Why It Cannot Be Skipped","section":"The two times","sectionId":"the-two-times","content":"The wird of the Karkariya is prescribed for morning and evening — *bushra wa ashiyya*. This mirrors the Quranic command: \"Remember your Lord much, and glorify Him morning and evening\" (3:41). Morning and evening are the hinges of the day. The wird at Fajr orients the whole waking day toward God. The wird at Maghrib seals the day and prepares the heart for the night."},{"url":"/articles/on-the-wird","title":"On the Wird: Why It Cannot Be Skipped","section":"When life intervenes","sectionId":"when-life-intervenes","content":"The Shaykh is not harsh about the rare missed occasion due to genuine circumstance. What he addresses is the attitude of negotiating with the wird — treating it as optional, as something to fit in when convenient. That attitude is the illness, not the occasional miss. > \\[!orbit]- color=orange\n> Q: What analogy does the Shaykh use when a student asks to do the wird every other day?\n> A: \"Do you eat every other day? Do you sleep every other day?\" — the heart needs daily sustenance just like the b"},{"url":"/articles/on-the-wird","title":"On the Wird: Why It Cannot Be Skipped","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Wird (Dictionary) First Pillar: Wird"},{"url":"/articles/style-test","title":"Style Test","content":"Test page for rough-notation annotations, sidenotes, and aliases — delete when done. This page exercises the reading affordances. Delete it when everything checks out."},{"url":"/articles/style-test","title":"Style Test","section":"Sidenotes & marginalia","sectionId":"sidenotes--marginalia","content":"The simplest form is a bare note in double braces.[^_sn_2] The text flows on as if nothing happened. Notes support inline markdown: emphasis, links, code.[^_sn_3] They can be as short[^_sn_4] or as long as a small paragraph.[^_sn_5] The labeled form keeps a phrase in the prose and hangs the note off it. The doctrine of spiritual poverty faqr[^_sn_1] is central to the path. Classic GFM footnotes render exactly the same way, so all three syntaxes coexist in one note."},{"url":"/articles/style-test","title":"Style Test","section":"Sources","sectionId":"sources","content":"This one was written as a standard `[^classic]` footnote — same rendering, no bottom footnote section. Double braces inside code are left alone: And neither is inline code like `{{date}}`. A final paragraph with one more note to test stacking near the end of the article.[^_sn_6] That's the whole tour."},{"url":"/articles/style-test","title":"Style Test","section":"Inline annotations","sectionId":"inline-annotations","content":"Here is a highlighted phrase inside a sentence. This one is underlined by hand, and this is boxed in orange. The engine can also draw a circle around words, or put brackets around a phrase like an editor's note. Two in one line: first mark and then second mark right after. An unclosed ==delimiter should render as plain text without breaking anything."},{"url":"/articles/style-test","title":"Style Test","section":"Annotations in headings are ==ignored==","sectionId":"annotations-in-headings-are-ignored","content":"The heading above must render its `==` literally — annotations are skipped inside headings."},{"url":"/articles/style-test","title":"Style Test","section":"Sidenotes","sectionId":"sidenotes","content":"Standard footnotes become margin sidenotes on wide screens, and collapse to click-to-expand inline notes on narrow ones. A second reference tests multi-paragraph footnote content, and the same note can even be referenced twice. The Wird is recited daily. A short sidenote. It should appear in the margin next to its reference number. A longer footnote with two paragraphs. The second paragraph should render as a separate block inside the sidenote, not break the page layout. Sidenotes can contain *f"},{"url":"/articles/style-test","title":"Style Test","section":"Callouts","sectionId":"callouts","content":"> \\[!note] Note\n> In the vault this is authored as `> [!note]`. The title is a small ledger label; the thread bar and hairline carry the type colour — gold for notes and quotes. > \\[!question] Question\n> Questions, help, and FAQ callouts take the violet patch from the muraqqaʿa spectrum. > \\[!warning] Warning\n> Warnings keep the Fumadocs warning token so they read consistently with the rest of the UI. > \\[!danger] Danger\n> Danger, bug, and failure callouts take the crimson patch. In dark mode ev"},{"url":"/articles/what-is-the-tariqa","title":"What is the Tariqa?","content":"A foundational Q&A in which the Shaykh explains what the Tariqa Karkariya is and is not. *Excerpt from a gathering in Fes. Lightly edited for clarity.* **Q: Shaykh, what exactly is the Tariqa Karkariya? Is it a new order or part of something older?** **A:** The Tariqa is not new. Nothing in it is invented. The Silsila goes back to the Prophet ﷺ, and every practice we do comes from that chain. What is \"new,\" if anything, is the name and the particular form in which this heritage is expressed in o"},{"url":"/base-test","title":"Base Test","content":"Published AFFiNE pages in Base Test."},{"url":"/base-test","title":"Base Test","section":"Base Test","sectionId":"base-test","content":"The Science of the Letters Stations of the Seekers The Niche of Lights The Alchemy of Happiness The Bezels of Wisdom Revival of the Religious Sciences The Conference of the Birds The Masnavi Principles of Sufism The Book of Assistance The Kashf al-Mahjub The Divan of Hafiz"},{"url":"/base-test/note-001","title":"The Science of the Letters","content":"The Science of the Letters A foundational treatise on the mystical significance of the Arabic alphabet."},{"url":"/base-test/note-002","title":"Stations of the Seekers","content":"Stations of the Seekers One hundred stations on the spiritual path, from repentance to annihilation."},{"url":"/base-test/note-003","title":"The Niche of Lights","content":"The Niche of Lights Al-Ghazali's mystical commentary on the Light Verse of the Quran."},{"url":"/base-test/note-004","title":"The Alchemy of Happiness","content":"The Alchemy of Happiness A Persian summary of the Ihya Ulum al-Din, focused on practical spirituality."},{"url":"/base-test/note-005","title":"The Bezels of Wisdom","content":"The Bezels of Wisdom Each chapter presents a facet of divine wisdom through the lens of a prophet."},{"url":"/base-test/note-006","title":"Revival of the Religious Sciences","content":"Revival of the Religious Sciences The magnum opus, covering outward acts of worship, inner vices, and the stations of the heart."},{"url":"/base-test/note-007","title":"The Conference of the Birds","content":"The Conference of the Birds An allegorical poem where thirty birds journey to find the Simurgh."},{"url":"/base-test/note-008","title":"The Masnavi","content":"The Masnavi Six books of rhyming couplets — called \"the Quran in Persian\" by Jami."},{"url":"/base-test/note-009","title":"Principles of Sufism","content":"Principles of Sufism A systematic introduction to Sufi terminology and the biographies of early masters."},{"url":"/base-test/note-010","title":"The Book of Assistance","content":"The Book of Assistance A concise manual of spiritual practice from the Ba-Alawi tradition of Hadramaut."},{"url":"/base-test/note-011","title":"The Kashf al-Mahjub","content":"The Kashf al-Mahjub The oldest Persian treatise on Sufism, covering doctrines and sects."},{"url":"/base-test/note-012","title":"The Divan of Hafiz","content":"The Divan of Hafiz The collected ghazals of the \"tongue of the unseen\" — the supreme lyric poet of Persian."},{"url":"/books/at-the-service-of-destiny","title":"At the Service of Destiny","content":"A biography of Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari — how a young orphan became a blacksmith of hearts, absorbed in the Divine Presence and occupied with the purification of hearts. God exalted says, \"I have fashioned thee for Myself.\" In His infinite wisdom, God willed for His Prophets and saints to assume the form of His command. He fashioned their lives as spiritual allusions to guide pure hearts to the disclosures of His direct knowledge. Nothing falls outside of His all-subjugating power, inclu"},{"url":"/books/candles-on-the-path","title":"Candles on the Path","content":"Excerpts from the teachings of Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari on spiritual guidance, visions of Light, and the practical wisdom of the Sufi path. Excerpts from the teachings of Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari. This book brings together notes from Sidi Shaykh's illuminating responses to questions posed by his disciples regarding their visions of Light and spiritual experiences. Translated with meticulous care to preserve the essence and authenticity of Sidi Shaykh's teachings, this book captur"},{"url":"/books/etiquette-of-spiritual-companionship","title":"Etiquette of Spiritual Companionship","content":"A study of the conditions for embarking on the spiritual journey with a divinely connected walī, and the gravity of the Shaykh's holy assembly. It is crucial for those who seek to reach the Divine Presence to carefully study the conditions for embarking on the journey with the divinely connected righteous walī. Through this preparation, one gains insight into their own self and the true aim of their heart when placing their foot in the presence of the Cupbearer (as-Sāqī), i.e., the Shaykh. One s"},{"url":"/books/guided-by-the-divine-light","title":"Guided By The Divine Light","content":"Notes from the Shaykh's lectures responding to disciples' questions about their visions of Light and spiritual experiences, across thirty chapters. Embark on an enlightening journey guided by the luminous lectures of Sidi Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari, may Allah sanctify his secret. This book brings together notes from Sidi Shaykh's illuminating responses to questions posed by his disciples regarding their visions of Light and spiritual experiences. Translated with meticulous care to preserve"},{"url":"/books/in-the-footsteps-of-moses","title":"In the Footsteps of Moses","content":"A contemporary Sufi commentary on the story of God's Confidant (kalīm Allāh) in the Qurʾān — Moses' birth, training under Shuʿayb, Mount Ṭūr, the Red Sea, and al-Khiḍr. Moses is one of the most revered Prophets in Islam. The fact that he is mentioned in the Qurʾān more than any other figure bespeaks his eminence and the significance of his prophetic narrative to spiritual wayfaring. His election by God and the unfolding events of his prophetic mission have served as a model for Sufi wayfaring. T"},{"url":"/books","title":"Books","content":"Published AFFiNE pages in Books."},{"url":"/books","title":"Books","section":"Books","sectionId":"books","content":"At the Service of Destiny Candles on the Path Etiquette of Spiritual Companionship Guided By The Divine Light In the Footsteps of Moses Introduction to Islamic Metaphysics Sufism Revived The Foundations of the Karkariya Order The Sufi Path of Light Wisdoms of the Heart Zamzam of Spiritual Knowledge"},{"url":"/books/introduction-to-islamic-metaphysics","title":"Introduction to Islamic Metaphysics","content":"A Sufi treatise mapping the mystical journey to God as an initiatic progression through seven degrees of realization of the divine Name Allāh. In this masterful treatise of Sufi spirituality and metaphysics, Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari maps out the mystical journey to God as an initiatic progression through seven degrees of realization, or readings, of the divine Name Allāh."},{"url":"/books/sufism-revived","title":"Sufism Revived","content":"A contemporary treatise on divine Light, prophecy, and sainthood — Sufi commentary on the degrees of prophethood (nubūwa), messengerhood (risāla), and sainthood (wilāya). In this compilation of spiritual discourses (sing. mudhākara), Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari offers a Sufi commentary on the functions, degrees and implications of prophethood (nubūwa), messengerhood (risāla), and sainthood (wilāya). Major themes include the identification of the Prophet Muhammad with \"the supreme intellect\""},{"url":"/books/the-foundations-of-the-karkariya-order","title":"The Foundations of the Karkariya Order","content":"The seven foundational principles of the spiritual path — the Pact, the Sacred Dance, the Patched Cloak, the Singular Name, Wandering, the Spiritual Retreat, and the Innermost Secret. The Foundations of the Karkariya Order articulates the seven foundational principles of the Spiritual Path as enshrined in the sacred Qurʾān and Hadith of the holy Prophet. These foundations consist of the Pact (al-ʿahd), the Sacred Dance (al-hadra), the Patched Cloak (al-muraqqa'a), the Singular Name (al-ism al-mu"},{"url":"/books/the-sufi-path-of-light","title":"The Sufi Path of Light","content":"A major contemporary treatise on divine Light in the Qurʾān, the Hadith, and the Sufi tradition, with commentary on the Light Verse and the aphorisms of Ibn ʿAṭāʾ Illāh. \"Our Lord is Light; our holy Prophet is Light; our unswerving Islamic faith is Light; our Holy Qurʾān is Light; and our prayer is Light. Why then do you wish to live in darkness? Why do you aloofly imagine, with your delimited and narrow mind, that the Light is merely an abstract concept that cannot be seen?\" inquires Shaykh Moh"},{"url":"/books/wisdoms-of-the-heart","title":"Wisdoms of the Heart","content":"A collection of mudhākara (spiritual discourses) on self-knowledge, the nafs, and the necessity of a living Shaykh for the seeker. Have you ever wondered about the world around you? Have you ever wondered about the reason of your existence? Have you ever wondered about Allah? Have you ever wondered about yourself? \"The one who knows his nafs knows his Lord.\" The goal is to know our nafs, to face its reality and to perfect it. However, if you want to be sure of the right direction, you need to fi"},{"url":"/books/zamzam-of-spiritual-knowledge","title":"Zamzam of Spiritual Knowledge","content":"A guide to how the Shaykh navigates the disciple through the body's vessels to the heart, where the divine secret — the disciple's Zamzam — is experienced. To understand how the Shaykh guides a disciple through their body, it is essential to know that all points of the body are interconnected through blood vessels, which, in turn, are all connected to the heart. The task of the accomplished Shaykh is to navigate the disciple through their body via these vessels until they reach their heart. Once"},{"url":"/canvas/getting-started","title":"Getting Started","content":"Getting Started Welcome to AFFiNE! You can start with a normal page, with richly formatted text, *markdown support*, links, and a lot of other blocks. Click anywhere to start typing Click to edit this line, and then drag the handle on the left to reorder blocks `/ `for more blocks `@ ` for linking and mentioning （`docs | users | dates`） e.g. select a date to leave a note for that day, view by date in Journals nested lists can be toggled to expand and collapse So is headings Click `+` in left nav"},{"url":"/canvas/getting-started","title":"Getting Started","section":"Data-intensive blocks","sectionId":"data-intensive-blocks","content":"**Table** Column Expandable Row Hover **here** to see drag handles"},{"url":"/canvas/getting-started","title":"Getting Started","section":"Examples for advanced blocks","sectionId":"examples-for-advanced-blocks","content":"For example, Latex math. To explore more, type ` /` To solve the equation  , follow these steps: Start by simplifying the equation: Add 1 to both sides to isolate the   term: Take the square root of both sides to solve for  : Therefore, the solutions to the equation are   and  ."},{"url":"/canvas/getting-started","title":"Getting Started","section":"Continue with the rabbit hole","sectionId":"continue-with-the-rabbit-hole","content":"Toggle the switch next to the title, to continue into the rabbit hole of intuitive organizational prowess \\~ !"},{"url":"/canvas/getting-started","title":"Getting Started","section":"And one more thing:","sectionId":"and-one-more-thing","content":"Links and Backlink support, they will assemble in their own section👇"},{"url":"/canvas/getting-started","title":"Getting Started","section":"Organize","sectionId":"organize","content":"folder sub-doc as well tag"},{"url":"/canvas/getting-started","title":"Getting Started","section":"Invite and collaborate","sectionId":"invite-and-collaborate","content":"invite mention comment(soon) share, co-edit or read only"},{"url":"/canvas/getting-started","title":"Getting Started","section":"AI","sectionId":"ai","content":"Login to give it a try Chat with your doc and workspace, and search with reasoning across the internet free 7-day trial !"},{"url":"/canvas/getting-started","title":"Getting Started","section":"Selfhost, check","sectionId":"selfhost-check","content":"Easy solution based on Docker Compose Google SSO ,etc supported in admin panel https\\://docs.affine.pro/docs/self-host-affine Rich link and embedding supported"},{"url":"/canvas/getting-started","title":"Getting Started","section":"GitHub and Discord","sectionId":"github-and-discord","content":"Code available (Consider Starring🌟) Discord channel"},{"url":"/canvas/getting-started","title":"Getting Started","section":"Cloud","sectionId":"cloud","content":"Sign in and enable cloud service to sync across devices"},{"url":"/canvas/getting-started","title":"Getting Started","section":"Importing Content?","sectionId":"importing-content","content":"Import from generic markdown files, or Notion Web Clipper Readwise integration https\\://www\\.youtube.com/watch?v=WqOe9HgpsDY https\\://affine.pro/"},{"url":"/canvas","title":"Canvas","content":"Published AFFiNE pages in Canvas."},{"url":"/canvas","title":"Canvas","section":"Canvas","sectionId":"canvas","content":"Getting Started"},{"url":"/dictionary/baya","title":"Bay'a","content":"The act of initiation into the Tariqa — the pledge of allegiance between murid and Shaykh. **Bay'a** (Arabic: بَيْعَة, \"pledge\" or \"allegiance\") is the formal act of initiation into the Tariqa Karkariya, in which the aspirant takes the hand of the Shaykh (or his representative) and pledges to follow the path under his guidance."},{"url":"/dictionary/baya","title":"Bay'a","section":"Meaning","sectionId":"meaning","content":"The word *bay'a* is the same used for the historical pledge of allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ. In the hadith of the tree (bay'at al-ridwan), companions pledged with the Prophet under a tree before the treaty of Hudaybiya. The Sufi bay'a is understood as a continuation of this covenant — each bay'a in the chain connecting back to that original pledge."},{"url":"/dictionary/baya","title":"Bay'a","section":"What happens in bay'a","sectionId":"what-happens-in-baya","content":"The aspirant states their intention The Shaykh (or authorized representative) takes their hand The Fatiha is recited The Shaykh breathes the wird formula (or its transmission) into the murid's heart The murid receives the Wird and instructions"},{"url":"/dictionary/baya","title":"Bay'a","section":"Who can give bay'a","sectionId":"who-can-give-baya","content":"In the Karkariya, bay'a is given by Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari or by authorized representatives (*muqaddams*) who have been granted this permission. Bay'a given by an unauthorized person is considered without effect."},{"url":"/dictionary/baya","title":"Bay'a","section":"Renewal","sectionId":"renewal","content":"Bay'a can be renewed — particularly when a murid visits the Shaykh in person — deepening the initial connection."},{"url":"/dictionary/baya","title":"Bay'a","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Murid Silsila Muqaddam"},{"url":"/dictionary/faqr","title":"Faqr","content":"Spiritual poverty — the station of total dependence on God and freedom from all but Him. **Faqr** (Arabic: فَقْر, \"poverty\") is one of the most celebrated stations of the Sufi path. It refers not to material poverty but to the spiritual state of total emptiness before God — the recognition that one possesses nothing, is nothing, and needs nothing except the divine."},{"url":"/dictionary/faqr","title":"Faqr","section":"Quranic basis","sectionId":"quranic-basis","content":"> \"O mankind, you are the poor (&#x2A;fuqara'*) toward God, and God — He is the Rich, the Praiseworthy.\" (Quran 35:15) The Sufi masters took this verse as a description of ultimate reality, not merely a metaphor."},{"url":"/dictionary/faqr","title":"Faqr","section":"The Shaykh on faqr","sectionId":"the-shaykh-on-faqr","content":"Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari has spoken extensively on faqr as the very identity of the path: > \"The tariqa is faqr — and faqr is the tariqa.\" In this understanding, the goal of all practice (wird, hadra, khalwa, etc.) is to arrive at this station of utter spiritual nakedness before God, which paradoxically becomes fullness."},{"url":"/dictionary/faqr","title":"Faqr","section":"Faqr and fana'","sectionId":"faqr-and-fana","content":"Faqr is intimately related to &#x2A;*fana'** (annihilation) — the dissolution of the ego-self in the divine presence. Where fana' describes the *event*, faqr describes the *station*: the permanent abiding in poverty-before-God."},{"url":"/dictionary/faqr","title":"Faqr","section":"The fuqara'","sectionId":"the-fuqara","content":"Members of Sufi orders are traditionally called &#x2A;*fuqara'** (pl. of *faqir*, \"poor one\") — not as an insult but as a title of honor, marking their commitment to this path."},{"url":"/dictionary/faqr","title":"Faqr","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Murid Khalwa Sirr"},{"url":"/dictionary/hadra","title":"Hadra","content":"The collective spiritual gathering of the Tariqa, combining dhikr, poetry, and presence. The **hadra** (Arabic: حَضْرَة, \"presence\") is the communal gathering of the Tariqa in which murids collectively perform dhikr, recite poetry in praise of the Prophet ﷺ, and enter states of spiritual presence (hudur). It is one of the seven foundational pillars of the Karkariya."},{"url":"/dictionary/hadra","title":"Hadra","section":"Form","sectionId":"form","content":"The hadra typically unfolds in stages: **Opening recitations** — Fatiha and salawat **Collective dhikr** — rhythmic remembrance, beginning slow and increasing in tempo **Inshad** — devotional poetry (qasaid), often from the Burda, Hamziyya, or compositions of the Shaykh **Hal** — the spiritual states that may arise among participants"},{"url":"/dictionary/hadra","title":"Hadra","section":"Setting","sectionId":"setting","content":"Hadras are held in the zawiya or designated meeting spaces. The Shaykh or a designated representative (muqaddam) leads the gathering. Men and women may gather separately or in designated arrangements depending on local custom."},{"url":"/dictionary/hadra","title":"Hadra","section":"Inner meaning","sectionId":"inner-meaning","content":"The word *hadra* itself points to the purpose: the \"presence\" refers not only to the gathering of people but to the sought-for spiritual presence of the Prophet ﷺ and, ultimately, the divine presence. The Shaykh teaches that the hadra is a school of the heart, not merely a ritual."},{"url":"/dictionary/hadra","title":"Hadra","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Wird Zikr Inshad"},{"url":"/dictionary","title":"Dictionary","content":"Published AFFiNE pages in Dictionary."},{"url":"/dictionary","title":"Dictionary","section":"Dictionary","sectionId":"dictionary","content":"Bay'a Faqr Hadra Ism al-Mufrad Khalwa Muraqqa' Murid Silsila Sirr Siyaha Wird Zikr"},{"url":"/dictionary/ism-al-mufrad","title":"Ism al-Mufrad","content":"The Singular Name — the dhikr of the divine name \"Allah\" as the heart's sole occupation. change **Ism al-Mufrad** (Arabic: اسم المُفرَد, \"the Singular Name\") refers to the dhikr of the divine name **Allah** (الله) alone — not as a phrase, but as a single name repeated with full presence of heart. It is one of the seven foundational pillars of the Tariqa Karkariya."},{"url":"/dictionary/ism-al-mufrad","title":"Ism al-Mufrad","section":"Distinction from other dhikr","sectionId":"distinction-from-other-dhikr","content":"Where the formula *La ilaha illa Allah* (the negation-affirmation) is the dhikr of the Wird, the Ism al-Mufrad is a more concentrated practice. It consists solely of the name **Allah**, breathed or spoken inwardly with each breath, until the name occupies the whole of the inner space. Shaykh al-Alawi, a major influence on the Karkariya's method, considered this dhikr the summit of the tariqas he had encountered."},{"url":"/dictionary/ism-al-mufrad","title":"Ism al-Mufrad","section":"Method","sectionId":"method","content":"The murid is instructed in the proper technique by the Shaykh or his representative. Generally: The name is pronounced with the lips, then increasingly with the heart alone Breath is coordinated with the name The gaze of the heart (basar al-qalb) is directed toward the meaning, not the letters"},{"url":"/dictionary/ism-al-mufrad","title":"Ism al-Mufrad","section":"Significance","sectionId":"significance","content":"The Ism al-Mufrad is considered the most direct path to fana' (annihilation) and baqa' (subsistence in God). The Shaykh has described it as \"the key that opens the lock of the heart.\""},{"url":"/dictionary/ism-al-mufrad","title":"Ism al-Mufrad","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Wird Muraqqa' Fana wa Baqa"},{"url":"/dictionary/khalwa","title":"Khalwa","content":"Spiritual retreat; a period of seclusion undertaken for intensive worship and purification. **Khalwa** (Arabic: خَلْوَة, \"seclusion\" or \"solitude\") is the practice of withdrawing from ordinary social life for a defined period to devote oneself entirely to worship, dhikr, and purification of the heart. It is one of the seven foundational pillars of the Tariqa Karkariya."},{"url":"/dictionary/khalwa","title":"Khalwa","section":"Historical roots","sectionId":"historical-roots","content":"Khalwa has roots in prophetic practice — the Prophet ﷺ would withdraw to the Cave of Hira before his prophethood. It became a central element of Sufi training across all major orders, including the Shadhiliyya lineage from which the Karkariya descends."},{"url":"/dictionary/khalwa","title":"Khalwa","section":"The Karkariya khalwa","sectionId":"the-karkariya-khalwa","content":"The Shaykh prescribes khalwa individually, based on the readiness and state of the murid. Key elements: A designated space (often the zawiya or a room prepared for the purpose) Minimal food and sleep Continuous dhikr, particularly the Ism al-Mufrad Recitation of the Wird Minimal speech with others Regular reporting to the Shaykh about visions and inner states"},{"url":"/dictionary/khalwa","title":"Khalwa","section":"Duration","sectionId":"duration","content":"Khalwas in the Karkariya may last from a few days to forty days (the traditional *arba'in*), depending on the murid's state and the Shaykh's guidance."},{"url":"/dictionary/khalwa","title":"Khalwa","section":"Caution","sectionId":"caution","content":"The Shaykh emphasizes that khalwa undertaken without proper guidance or preparation is dangerous. The isolation can amplify not only spiritual openings but also the play of the ego and illusion. The presence of the Shaykh's guidance is essential."},{"url":"/dictionary/khalwa","title":"Khalwa","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Muraqqa' Sirr Siyaha"},{"url":"/dictionary/muraqqa","title":"Muraqqa'","content":"Spiritual watchfulness and contemplative meditation; the practice of inner vigilance. **Muraqqa'** (Arabic: مُرَاقَبَة, *muraqaba*) means watchfulness, vigilance, or contemplative awareness. In the Karkariya, it is one of the seven foundational pillars and refers to the sustained inward attention of the heart to the divine presence."},{"url":"/dictionary/muraqqa","title":"Muraqqa'","section":"Meaning","sectionId":"meaning","content":"The root *r-q-b* carries the sense of watching over, guarding, and being observed. Muraqaba is the practice of being aware that one is always under divine observation — and of orienting the heart continuously toward that reality. > \"Worship Allah as though you see Him; if you do not see Him, He sees you.\" — Hadith Jibril"},{"url":"/dictionary/muraqqa","title":"Muraqqa'","section":"Practice","sectionId":"practice","content":"Muraqaba in the Karkariya is not passive. It involves: Sitting in stillness with closed eyes Directing the heart toward a specific divine name or attribute, as instructed by the Shaykh Observing thoughts, images, and inner states without attachment Sustaining the connection (rabita) with the Shaykh inwardly Advanced muraqaba may involve specific contemplations (mushahada) assigned individually by the Shaykh."},{"url":"/dictionary/muraqqa","title":"Muraqqa'","section":"Relation to other practices","sectionId":"relation-to-other-practices","content":"Muraqaba deepens the fruits of the Wird and Hadra. Where dhikr moves the tongue and heart, muraqaba stills both to receive what dhikr has opened."},{"url":"/dictionary/muraqqa","title":"Muraqqa'","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Ism al-Mufrad Khalwa Sirr"},{"url":"/dictionary/murid","title":"Murid","content":"The spiritual aspirant who has taken initiation (bay'a) into the Tariqa. **Murid** (Arabic: مُريد, \"one who desires\" or \"one who wills\") is the term for a person who has formally entered the Tariqa through the act of initiation (Bay'a). The word derives from *irada* — will or intention — signifying that the murid has made a deliberate choice to travel the path under the guidance of a Shaykh."},{"url":"/dictionary/murid","title":"Murid","section":"The murid's obligations","sectionId":"the-murids-obligations","content":"Upon taking bay'a, the murid undertakes: Daily recitation of the Wird Attendance at the Hadra when possible Obedience to the Shaykh's guidance (*ta'a*) Sincere repentance (*tawba*) and avoidance of major sins Regular spiritual accounting (*muhasaba*)"},{"url":"/dictionary/murid","title":"Murid","section":"The murid's relationship with the Shaykh","sectionId":"the-murids-relationship-with-the-shaykh","content":"The Karkariya teaching places great emphasis on the inner connection (rabita) between murid and Shaykh. The Shaykh serves as a spiritual physician — diagnosing the diseases of the soul and prescribing remedies from the treasury of the Tariqa. The murid's trust (*tawakkul*) in this relationship is foundational. > \"The murid before his Shaykh is like the body in the hands of the washer.\" — classical Sufi saying"},{"url":"/dictionary/murid","title":"Murid","section":"Levels","sectionId":"levels","content":"Not all murids are at the same stage. The Tariqa distinguishes between the beginning murid (&#x2A;mubtadi'*), the intermediate (*mutawassit*), and the advanced (*muntahi*) — though these are internal states, not formal ranks."},{"url":"/dictionary/murid","title":"Murid","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Bay'a Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari Wird"},{"url":"/dictionary/silsila","title":"Silsila","content":"The initiatic chain of transmission connecting the present Shaykh back to the Prophet ﷺ through an unbroken line of masters. **Silsila** (Arabic: سِلسِلَة, \"chain\") is the unbroken chain of spiritual transmission linking each master of the Tariqa to his predecessor, and ultimately to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. It is the guarantee of the authenticity and living power of the path."},{"url":"/dictionary/silsila","title":"Silsila","section":"The Karkariya silsila","sectionId":"the-karkariya-silsila","content":"The Tariqa Karkariya traces its lineage through the following principal links: **The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ** **Sayyidna Ali ibn Abi Talib** (and via Abu Bakr al-Siddiq) ... &#x2A;(continuous chain through the centuries)* **Abd al-Salam ibn Mashish** (d. 1228) — the Moroccan pole **Abu al-Hasan al-Shadhili** (d. 1258) — founder of the Shadhiliyya **Abu al-Abbas al-Mursi** (d. 1287) **Ibn Ata Allah al-Iskandari** (d. 1309) ... &#x2A;(Shadhili chain continues)* **Sidi Ahmad ibn Idris al-Fasi** (d. 183"},{"url":"/dictionary/silsila","title":"Silsila","section":"Significance","sectionId":"significance","content":"The silsila is not merely biographical. In the Sufi understanding, the baraka (spiritual blessing) of the Prophet ﷺ flows through these human channels like light through a chain of lamps. Taking the hand of the Shaykh (*bay'a*) connects the murid to this living current."},{"url":"/dictionary/silsila","title":"Silsila","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Bay'a Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari Ahmad al-Alawi Abd al-Salam ibn Mashish"},{"url":"/dictionary/sirr","title":"Sirr","content":"The innermost secret of the heart; the deepest faculty of the human being, where divine disclosure occurs. **Sirr** (Arabic: سِرّ, \"secret\" or \"innermost\") refers to the most inward faculty of the human being — deeper than the *qalb* (heart) and *ruh* (spirit). It is in the sirr that divine disclosure (*kashf*) and intimate conversation (*munajat*) with the divine occur. It is one of the seven foundational pillars of the Tariqa Karkariya."},{"url":"/dictionary/sirr","title":"Sirr","section":"The subtle centers (lata'if)","sectionId":"the-subtle-centers-lataif","content":"Classical Sufi anthropology describes the human being as having several subtle spiritual centers (*lata'if*, sing. *latifa*): Center Arabic Locus Nafs نفس Lower self Qalb قلب Heart Ruh روح Spirit Sirr سرّ Innermost Khafi خفي The hidden Akhfa أخفى The most hidden The Karkariya's method involves the progressive illumination of these centers through dhikr and the guidance of the Shaykh."},{"url":"/dictionary/sirr","title":"Sirr","section":"Sirr as a pillar","sectionId":"sirr-as-a-pillar","content":"As a foundational practice, \"Sirr\" in the context of the Karkariya's seven pillars refers to the cultivation and guarding of this innermost faculty — keeping it sealed from the world and open only toward God. The murid is taught to recognize and protect the sirr from dispersion through excessive speech, sensory indulgence, and attachment."},{"url":"/dictionary/sirr","title":"Sirr","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Muraqqa' Khalwa Ism al-Mufrad"},{"url":"/dictionary/siyaha","title":"Siyaha","content":"Spiritual wandering or journey; the practice of leaving one's familiar world for the sake of God. **Siyaha** (Arabic: سِيَاحَة, \"wandering\" or \"traveling\") is one of the seven foundational pillars of the Tariqa Karkariya. It refers to spiritual travel undertaken for the sake of God — visiting the Shaykh, attending hadras in distant places, or undertaking journeys of sacred pilgrimage and spiritual intent."},{"url":"/dictionary/siyaha","title":"Siyaha","section":"Meaning","sectionId":"meaning","content":"The root *s-y-h* suggests flowing water and wide movement. In the Sufi context, siyaha is the outward correlate of the inward journey: just as the heart must leave behind its attachments to travel toward God, the body may be called to leave behind familiar comfort. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Travel, for you will be healthy and provided for.\" The Sufi tradition read this on multiple levels."},{"url":"/dictionary/siyaha","title":"Siyaha","section":"Forms","sectionId":"forms","content":"**Visiting the Shaykh** — the murid's journey to be in the physical presence of the guide **Attending gatherings** — traveling to a hadra or ziyara (visit to a saint's tomb) **Khalwa travel** — relocating to a new place for a period of spiritual retreat (Khalwa) **Siyaha of the heart** — the inward dimension: detachment from the world's pull"},{"url":"/dictionary/siyaha","title":"Siyaha","section":"In the Karkariya today","sectionId":"in-the-karkariya-today","content":"Murids of the Karkariya travel to Morocco — particularly to Fes and the Shaykh's zawiya — for the major annual gatherings and to renew their connection. These journeys are considered a form of siyaha."},{"url":"/dictionary/siyaha","title":"Siyaha","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Khalwa Hadra Silsila"},{"url":"/dictionary/wird","title":"Wird","content":"The daily litany of the Tariqa Karkariya, recited morning and evening. The **wird** (Arabic: وِرْد, pl. *awrad*) is the foundational daily litany assigned to every murid upon entry into the Tariqa. It consists of a sequence of formulas of remembrance (dhikr), prayers upon the Prophet (salawat), and Quranic recitations performed at fixed times — typically at dawn and after the Asr or Maghrib prayer."},{"url":"/dictionary/wird","title":"Wird","section":"Structure","sectionId":"structure","content":"The wird of the Karkariya follows the classical Shadhili pattern, drawn from the lineage through Sidi Ahmad ibn Idris and Shaykh Ahmad al-Alawi. It includes: **Istighfar** — seeking forgiveness (100×) **Salat al-Fatih** — the opening prayer upon the Prophet **La ilaha illa Allah** — the declaration of divine unity (100×) **Salat al-Mashishiyya** — the prayer of Ibn Mashish"},{"url":"/dictionary/wird","title":"Wird","section":"Significance","sectionId":"significance","content":"The wird is considered the lifeline of the murid's connection to the Shaykh and, through him, to the Prophet ﷺ. Missing it without excuse is considered a serious spiritual lapse. The Shaykh has described it as \"the rope between the servant and his Lord.\""},{"url":"/dictionary/wird","title":"Wird","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Zikr Silsila Murid nice"},{"url":"/dictionary/zikr","title":"Zikr","content":"The remembrance of God; the central practice of all Sufi orders, taking many forms in the Karkariya. **Zikr** (also *dhikr*, Arabic: ذِكْر, \"remembrance\" or \"mention\") is the practice of remembering God through the repeated utterance of His names, attributes, or formulas of praise. It is the central practice of all Sufi orders and the engine of the spiritual life in the Karkariya."},{"url":"/dictionary/zikr","title":"Zikr","section":"Quranic basis","sectionId":"quranic-basis","content":"> \"Remember God with much remembrance\" (Quran 33:41) > \"Verily in the remembrance of God do hearts find rest.\" (Quran 13:28) The Quran commands zikr more frequently than almost any other act of worship. The Sufi tradition took this mandate seriously and developed elaborate sciences of its application."},{"url":"/dictionary/zikr","title":"Zikr","section":"Forms in the Karkariya","sectionId":"forms-in-the-karkariya","content":"Form Description Zikr al-lisan Verbal remembrance with the tongue Zikr al-qalb Remembrance with the heart Zikr al-sirr Remembrance in the Sirr — the innermost Zikr al-ruh Remembrance of the spirit Zikr al-khafi The hidden remembrance"},{"url":"/dictionary/zikr","title":"Zikr","section":"Individual and collective","sectionId":"individual-and-collective","content":"The Wird is the individual daily zikr. The Hadra is the collective zikr. Both are essential — one trains the private relationship with God, the other the communal fabric of the Tariqa."},{"url":"/dictionary/zikr","title":"Zikr","section":"The Ism al-Mufrad","sectionId":"the-ism-al-mufrad","content":"The singular name *Allah* represents the summit of dhikr practice in the Karkariya method — the point where all formulas dissolve into pure presence."},{"url":"/dictionary/zikr","title":"Zikr","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Wird Hadra Ism al-Mufrad"},{"url":"/foundations/hadra","title":"Second Pillar: Hadra","content":"The collective spiritual gathering as a foundational pillar of the Tariqa Karkariya. The **Hadra** is the second of the seven pillars of the Tariqa Karkariya. See Hadra in the Dictionary for the full definition."},{"url":"/foundations/hadra","title":"Second Pillar: Hadra","section":"The hadra as pillar","sectionId":"the-hadra-as-pillar","content":"Where the wird is the individual's daily anchor, the hadra is the communal heartbeat of the Tariqa. It is the gathering that makes a dispersed community of individuals into a body. The Shaykh has said that the murid who lives far from a zawiya and cannot attend regular hadras should seek other murids and organize one, even if small. Two or three gathered in remembrance is a hadra."},{"url":"/foundations/hadra","title":"Second Pillar: Hadra","section":"The prophetic assembly","sectionId":"the-prophetic-assembly","content":"The hadra is modeled on the assemblies (*majalis*) of the Prophet ﷺ — gatherings where remembrance, recitation, and presence of the divine were the atmosphere. The companions described these gatherings as \"gardens of paradise.\""},{"url":"/foundations/hadra","title":"Second Pillar: Hadra","section":"Obligation and encouragement","sectionId":"obligation-and-encouragement","content":"The hadra is not obligatory in the same legal sense as prayer, but the Shaykh treats it as close to that in spiritual terms for murids who have access to it. Missing it without reason is considered a weakening of the murid's connection to the community and to the baraka."},{"url":"/foundations/hadra","title":"Second Pillar: Hadra","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Hadra (Dictionary) First Pillar: Wird Third Pillar: Muraqqa'"},{"url":"/foundations","title":"Foundations","content":"Published AFFiNE pages in Foundations."},{"url":"/foundations","title":"Foundations","section":"Foundations","sectionId":"foundations","content":"Second Pillar: Hadra Fourth Pillar: Ism al-Mufrad Sixth Pillar: Khalwa Third Pillar: Muraqqa' Seventh Pillar: Sirr Fifth Pillar: Siyaha First Pillar: Wird"},{"url":"/foundations/ism-al-mufrad","title":"Fourth Pillar: Ism al-Mufrad","content":"The Singular Name as a foundational pillar of the Tariqa Karkariya. The **Ism al-Mufrad** is the fourth of the seven pillars. See Ism al-Mufrad in the Dictionary."},{"url":"/foundations/ism-al-mufrad","title":"Fourth Pillar: Ism al-Mufrad","section":"Centrality","sectionId":"centrality","content":"Shaykh al-Alawi, whose method the Karkariya inherits, considered the dhikr of the singular name *Allah* to be the crowning practice of the path. The Shaykh has continued this emphasis, describing it as the practice that \"burns the veils\" between the servant and the Lord."},{"url":"/foundations/ism-al-mufrad","title":"Fourth Pillar: Ism al-Mufrad","section":"Transmission","sectionId":"transmission","content":"The method of the Ism al-Mufrad is transmitted directly from Shaykh to murid. This transmission (*talqin*) is itself considered a spiritual act — the Shaykh breathes the name into the heart of the murid through a specific method, charging it with the baraka of the Silsila."},{"url":"/foundations/ism-al-mufrad","title":"Fourth Pillar: Ism al-Mufrad","section":"Without transmission","sectionId":"without-transmission","content":"The Shaykh warns against practicing this dhikr without proper transmission and guidance. The concentrated power of the name, practiced incorrectly or without supervision, can lead to spiritual disturbance. The guide is the safety."},{"url":"/foundations/ism-al-mufrad","title":"Fourth Pillar: Ism al-Mufrad","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Ism al-Mufrad (Dictionary) Fifth Pillar: Siyaha"},{"url":"/foundations/khalwa","title":"Sixth Pillar: Khalwa","content":"Spiritual retreat as a foundational pillar of the Tariqa Karkariya. The **Khalwa** is the sixth of the seven pillars. See Khalwa in the Dictionary."},{"url":"/foundations/khalwa","title":"Sixth Pillar: Khalwa","section":"When khalwa is given","sectionId":"when-khalwa-is-given","content":"The Shaykh does not prescribe khalwa to every murid. It is given when the murid has demonstrated sufficient stability in the earlier pillars — consistent wird, regular hadra attendance, practising muraqaba — and when the Shaykh discerns that the murid is ready for the intensification that khalwa brings. Premature khalwa can do harm. The Shaykh is explicit about this."},{"url":"/foundations/khalwa","title":"Sixth Pillar: Khalwa","section":"What changes in khalwa","sectionId":"what-changes-in-khalwa","content":"Murids who have completed a khalwa describe a qualitative shift in their inner experience — a deepening of the Muraqqa' state, a greater ease in the Ism al-Mufrad, and often a clarification of their spiritual state and direction."},{"url":"/foundations/khalwa","title":"Sixth Pillar: Khalwa","section":"Modern khalwa","sectionId":"modern-khalwa","content":"For murids in Western contexts, the logistics of a forty-day khalwa are challenging. The Shaykh has given guidance on adapted forms — shorter retreats, intensified periods within ordinary life. The principle, not the duration, is what matters."},{"url":"/foundations/khalwa","title":"Sixth Pillar: Khalwa","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Khalwa (Dictionary) Seventh Pillar: Sirr"},{"url":"/foundations/muraqqa","title":"Third Pillar: Muraqqa'","content":"Spiritual watchfulness as a foundational pillar of the Tariqa Karkariya. The &#x2A;*Muraqqa'** is the third of the seven pillars. See Muraqqa' in the Dictionary."},{"url":"/foundations/muraqqa","title":"Third Pillar: Muraqqa'","section":"Muraqqa' as a daily practice","sectionId":"muraqqa-as-a-daily-practice","content":"Unlike the Wird which has fixed times, muraqaba is ideally a continuous state that is also practiced in formal sitting. The Shaykh prescribes dedicated times for muraqaba — typically after Fajr and after 'Isha — but the deeper aspiration is that the state of inner watchfulness become unbroken."},{"url":"/foundations/muraqqa","title":"Third Pillar: Muraqqa'","section":"The inner teacher","sectionId":"the-inner-teacher","content":"The Shaykh teaches that muraqaba, properly practiced, activates an inner guidance that begins to correct the murid from within. Rather than always needing external instruction, the murid starts to feel a pull away from what harms the heart and toward what nourishes it. This is the beginning of the *kashf* (unveiling)."},{"url":"/foundations/muraqqa","title":"Third Pillar: Muraqqa'","section":"Stages","sectionId":"stages","content":"**Muraqabat al-af'al** — watchfulness of actions **Muraqabat al-ahwal** — watchfulness of states **Muraqabat al-qalb** — watchfulness of the heart's movements **Muraqabat al-sirr** — watchfulness in the Sirr"},{"url":"/foundations/muraqqa","title":"Third Pillar: Muraqqa'","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Muraqqa' (Dictionary) Fourth Pillar: Ism al-Mufrad"},{"url":"/foundations/sirr","title":"Seventh Pillar: Sirr","content":"The innermost secret as the seventh and culminating pillar of the Tariqa Karkariya. The **Sirr** is the seventh and culminating pillar of the Tariqa Karkariya. See Sirr in the Dictionary."},{"url":"/foundations/sirr","title":"Seventh Pillar: Sirr","section":"The goal of the path","sectionId":"the-goal-of-the-path","content":"If the earlier six pillars are the ladder, the sirr is what is reached at the top. The entire architecture of the Karkariya method — wird, hadra, muraqaba, ism al-mufrad, siyaha, khalwa — serves the activation and purification of the sirr, the innermost faculty where the divine disclosure (*kashf*) and intimate witnessing (*mushahada*) occur."},{"url":"/foundations/sirr","title":"Seventh Pillar: Sirr","section":"Guarding the sirr","sectionId":"guarding-the-sirr","content":"One of the Shaykh's most consistent teachings is the *guarding of the sirr* — the practice of not speaking freely about one's spiritual experiences, visions, and states. The sirr is not to be displayed. When it is exposed, it leaks and diminishes. > \"The secret that is spoken is lost.\" — Karkariya teaching"},{"url":"/foundations/sirr","title":"Seventh Pillar: Sirr","section":"The sirr and the Shaykh","sectionId":"the-sirr-and-the-shaykh","content":"There is a dimension of the sirr that belongs to the relationship between murid and Shaykh. The Shaykh has access to the murid's inner state in a way that the murid themselves may not fully understand. This is why the relationship is built on trust and why the murid must be honest with the Shaykh even when it is difficult."},{"url":"/foundations/sirr","title":"Seventh Pillar: Sirr","section":"Completion","sectionId":"completion","content":"The seven pillars are not sequential stages that are left behind. They are simultaneous dimensions of the path — each feeding the others, all serving the emergence of the sirr into its fullness."},{"url":"/foundations/sirr","title":"Seventh Pillar: Sirr","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Sirr (Dictionary) First Pillar: Wird"},{"url":"/foundations/siyaha","title":"Fifth Pillar: Siyaha","content":"Spiritual wandering as a foundational pillar of the Tariqa Karkariya. The **Siyaha** is the fifth of the seven pillars. See Siyaha in the Dictionary."},{"url":"/foundations/siyaha","title":"Fifth Pillar: Siyaha","section":"Siyaha and detachment","sectionId":"siyaha-and-detachment","content":"The spiritual purpose of siyaha is not tourism. It is the practical training of detachment — learning that one can uproot and move, that home is not in a place but in God. This is why the Sufi masters valued travel even when difficult. The Shaykh has said: \"The murid who cannot leave his city for the sake of God is still attached. Siyaha cuts that rope.\""},{"url":"/foundations/siyaha","title":"Fifth Pillar: Siyaha","section":"Visits to Morocco","sectionId":"visits-to-morocco","content":"For murids outside Morocco, the journey to Fes and the Shaykh's zawiya is a major form of siyaha. These journeys — especially for the annual gathering (*mawsim*) — are spiritually charged events that murids describe as transformative."},{"url":"/foundations/siyaha","title":"Fifth Pillar: Siyaha","section":"Inner siyaha","sectionId":"inner-siyaha","content":"The outward dimension has an inward correlate: the willingness of the heart to be moved, displaced, and emptied. A murid stuck in routine, unable to be surprised by God's action, needs the practice of siyaha in its inner dimension."},{"url":"/foundations/siyaha","title":"Fifth Pillar: Siyaha","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Siyaha (Dictionary) Sixth Pillar: Khalwa"},{"url":"/foundations/wird","title":"First Pillar: Wird","content":"The daily litany as a foundational pillar of the Tariqa Karkariya. The **Wird** is the first of the seven pillars of the Tariqa Karkariya. See Wird in the Dictionary for the full definition."},{"url":"/foundations/wird","title":"First Pillar: Wird","section":"The wird as pillar","sectionId":"the-wird-as-pillar","content":"The Shaykh has described the wird as the foundation of the house — without it, nothing stands. Every other practice (hadra, khalwa, siyaha) rests on the daily habit of the wird. A murid who abandons the wird has, in effect, stepped off the path. The Shaykh distinguishes between missing it through genuine unavoidable circumstance (travel, illness) — for which there is latitude — and abandoning it through negligence, which severs the inner connection."},{"url":"/foundations/wird","title":"First Pillar: Wird","section":"Consistency over intensity","sectionId":"consistency-over-intensity","content":"The Karkariya teaching emphasizes consistency over the intensity of any single sitting. A brief wird performed daily is worth more than an extraordinary session followed by weeks of absence. This principle — *al-istimrar* (continuity) — runs through all the pillars."},{"url":"/foundations/wird","title":"First Pillar: Wird","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Wird (Dictionary) Second Pillar: Hadra"},{"url":"/history/abd-al-salam-ibn-mashish","title":"Abd al-Salam ibn Mashish","content":"The Moroccan pole (qutb) and master of Abu al-Hasan al-Shadhili; a pivotal link in the Karkariya silsila. **Abd al-Salam ibn Mashish** (Arabic: عبد السلام بن مشيش, d. 625 AH / 1228 CE) is one of the great poles (*aqtab*) of the Sufi tradition and the master of Abu al-Hasan al-Shadhili, the founder of the Shadhiliyya from which the Karkariya ultimately descends."},{"url":"/history/abd-al-salam-ibn-mashish","title":"Abd al-Salam ibn Mashish","section":"Life","sectionId":"life","content":"Ibn Mashish lived as a recluse on Jabal 'Alam in northern Morocco (near present-day Tetouan), a mountain that became a place of pilgrimage. He was known for extraordinary spiritual states and for receiving visitors from across the Islamic world who sought his blessing and guidance."},{"url":"/history/abd-al-salam-ibn-mashish","title":"Abd al-Salam ibn Mashish","section":"The famous prayer","sectionId":"the-famous-prayer","content":"Ibn Mashish composed the **Salat al-Mashishiyya** (صلاة المشيشية), one of the most celebrated prayers upon the Prophet ﷺ in Islamic history, which remains part of the spiritual repertoire of the Karkariya and countless other paths. Its opening lines describe the Prophet as the ocean of divine lights from which all existence flows. > \"O God, send blessings upon him from whom the secrets burst forth and the lights overflow\\...\""},{"url":"/history/abd-al-salam-ibn-mashish","title":"Abd al-Salam ibn Mashish","section":"Encounter with al-Shadhili","sectionId":"encounter-with-al-shadhili","content":"When Abu al-Hasan al-Shadhili came to Ibn Mashish seeking a master, he was instructed to first ritually wash himself of all worldly knowledge. After this symbolic purification, Ibn Mashish took his hand and gave him the wird, saying: \"Now you are ready.\""},{"url":"/history/abd-al-salam-ibn-mashish","title":"Abd al-Salam ibn Mashish","section":"His tomb","sectionId":"his-tomb","content":"The tomb of Ibn Mashish on Jabal 'Alam is a major site of *ziyara* (visitation) in Morocco, visited by hundreds of thousands annually."},{"url":"/history/abd-al-salam-ibn-mashish","title":"Abd al-Salam ibn Mashish","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Silsila Abu al-Hasan al-Shadhili Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari"},{"url":"/history/abu-al-hasan-al-shadhili","title":"Abu al-Hasan al-Shadhili","content":"Founder of the Shadhiliyya order and the central ancestral figure of the Karkariya lineage. **Abu al-Hasan Ali ibn Abdallah al-Shadhili** (Arabic: أبو الحسن الشاذلي, c. 593–656 AH / 1196–1258 CE) was a Moroccan-born Sufi master who founded the **Shadhiliyya** order — the vast family of Sufi paths from which the Karkariya descends."},{"url":"/history/abu-al-hasan-al-shadhili","title":"Abu al-Hasan al-Shadhili","section":"Life","sectionId":"life","content":"Born near Ceuta (in present-day northern Morocco), al-Shadhili traveled extensively in search of a master, eventually finding his guide in Abd al-Salam ibn Mashish on Jabal 'Alam. After his initiation, he journeyed to Ifriqiya (Tunisia) and then settled in Alexandria, Egypt, where he gathered a major circle of disciples. He died during the Hajj pilgrimage at Humaythara in the Egyptian desert in 1258 CE."},{"url":"/history/abu-al-hasan-al-shadhili","title":"Abu al-Hasan al-Shadhili","section":"Teaching style","sectionId":"teaching-style","content":"Al-Shadhili was famous for teaching in the marketplace, not in isolated retreats. He emphasized that the Sufi path was compatible with ordinary professional and family life — that the inward journey did not require withdrawal from the world. This teaching shaped the entire Shadhili tradition. > \"Do not carry the burdens of the world, for this is the Lord's business. Your duty is the path.\""},{"url":"/history/abu-al-hasan-al-shadhili","title":"Abu al-Hasan al-Shadhili","section":"The Shadhili method","sectionId":"the-shadhili-method","content":"Key emphases of al-Shadhili's method that carry through to the Karkariya: Trust in God (*tawakkul*) without abandoning worldly work The importance of the Wird as daily foundation Sincere repentance and moral rectification before mystical aspiration The centrality of the Prophet ﷺ as model and intermediary"},{"url":"/history/abu-al-hasan-al-shadhili","title":"Abu al-Hasan al-Shadhili","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Abd al-Salam ibn Mashish Ahmad al-Alawi Silsila"},{"url":"/history/ahmad-al-alawi","title":"Ahmad al-Alawi","content":"The Algerian master whose method the Karkariya most directly inherits; a towering figure of 20th-century Islam. **Shaykh Ahmad ibn Mustafa al-Alawi** (Arabic: أحمد العلوي, 1869–1934 CE) was an Algerian Sufi master of Moroccan descent, founder of the **Alawiyya** order and one of the most influential Islamic figures of the twentieth century. The Tariqa Karkariya inherits its method most directly through him."},{"url":"/history/ahmad-al-alawi","title":"Ahmad al-Alawi","section":"Life","sectionId":"life","content":"Born in Mostaganem, Algeria, al-Alawi first worked as a cobbler and practiced various folk religious traditions before encountering the Shadhili-Darqawi path under Shaykh Muhammad al-Buzidi, who introduced him to the dhikr of the Ism al-Mufrad. This encounter transformed him. After al-Buzidi's death, al-Alawi became an independent master and founded the Alawiyya, which spread across North and West Africa, the Middle East, and eventually to European Muslim communities."},{"url":"/history/ahmad-al-alawi","title":"Ahmad al-Alawi","section":"Influence on the Karkariya","sectionId":"influence-on-the-karkariya","content":"The Karkariya's distinctive emphasis on the Ism al-Mufrad, the method of Muraqqa', and the structure of the seven pillars draws directly from the Alawiyya tradition. Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari has described al-Alawi as \"the greatest Shaykh of our age.\""},{"url":"/history/ahmad-al-alawi","title":"Ahmad al-Alawi","section":"European encounter","sectionId":"european-encounter","content":"Al-Alawi was one of the first Sufi masters to have significant contact with European seekers. The French scholar Martin Lings (Abu Bakr Siraj al-Din) wrote an influential biography of him: *A Sufi Saint of the Twentieth Century*."},{"url":"/history/ahmad-al-alawi","title":"Ahmad al-Alawi","section":"Written works","sectionId":"written-works","content":"Al-Alawi was a prolific writer. His major works include: *Minah al-Quddusiyya* — on Sufi stations *Al-Qawl al-Ma'ruf* — responses to critics *Diwan* — collected poetry"},{"url":"/history/ahmad-al-alawi","title":"Ahmad al-Alawi","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Abu al-Hasan al-Shadhili Silsila Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari"},{"url":"/history","title":"History","content":"Published AFFiNE pages in History."},{"url":"/history","title":"History","section":"History","sectionId":"history","content":"Abd al-Salam ibn Mashish Abu al-Hasan al-Shadhili Ahmad al-Alawi Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari Origins of the Tariqa Karkariya"},{"url":"/history/shaykh-faouzi-al-karkari","title":"Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari","content":"The present guide of the Tariqa Karkariya, born 1974 in Fes, Morocco. **Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari** (Arabic: محمد فوزي الكركري) was born in **Fes, Morocco in 1974** and is the present guide (*murshid*) of the Tariqa Karkariya. He received his spiritual formation within the Shadhili-Alawi tradition and was designated as a master by his own guide before establishing the Karkariya as a distinct expression of that lineage."},{"url":"/history/shaykh-faouzi-al-karkari","title":"Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari","section":"Background","sectionId":"background","content":"Shaykh al-Karkari was raised in Fes, the spiritual capital of Morocco and home of the zawiya of Moulay Idris, the tomb of Abd al-Salam ibn Mashish's spiritual descendants, and centuries of continuous Sufi life. He memorized the Quran and received a traditional Islamic education alongside his Sufi formation."},{"url":"/history/shaykh-faouzi-al-karkari","title":"Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari","section":"The name \"Karkariya\"","sectionId":"the-name-karkariya","content":"The name derives from *karkara* — a sound of flowing water or gentle movement — reflecting the Shaykh's teaching that the path moves like water: without forcing, finding the natural channel, wearing away obstacles gradually."},{"url":"/history/shaykh-faouzi-al-karkari","title":"Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari","section":"Teaching approach","sectionId":"teaching-approach","content":"The Shaykh is known for: Accessibility to murids across the world, including Western converts Emphasis on the compatibility of the path with ordinary professional life Deep engagement with classical Sufi texts alongside oral transmission A warm and direct teaching style that makes difficult spiritual concepts approachable"},{"url":"/history/shaykh-faouzi-al-karkari","title":"Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari","section":"Global community","sectionId":"global-community","content":"Through travel and the internet, the Karkariya has gathered murids across Morocco, Europe, North America, and beyond. The Shaykh visits murids in their home countries and holds major gatherings in Fes."},{"url":"/history/shaykh-faouzi-al-karkari","title":"Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari","section":"Published works","sectionId":"published-works","content":"The Shaykh has authored and authorized several works on the Karkariya method, including collections of his teachings and explanations of the Wird and the seven pillars."},{"url":"/history/shaykh-faouzi-al-karkari","title":"Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Silsila Ahmad al-Alawi Bay'a"},{"url":"/history/tariqa-origins","title":"Origins of the Tariqa Karkariya","content":"How the Karkariya emerged from the Shadhili-Alawi lineage in contemporary Morocco. The **Tariqa Karkariya** is a contemporary Sufi order rooted in the **Shadhili-Alawi** tradition of North Africa. It was established by Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari in Fes, Morocco, in the late twentieth century."},{"url":"/history/tariqa-origins","title":"Origins of the Tariqa Karkariya","section":"Lineage summary","sectionId":"lineage-summary","content":"The Karkariya's roots reach back through a chain of masters: Master Period Role Abu al-Hasan al-Shadhili 1196–1258 Founder of the Shadhiliyya Abu al-Abbas al-Mursi 1219–1287 First successor Ibn Ata Allah al-Iskandari d. 1309 Author, systematizer ... ... Chain continues Sidi Ahmad ibn Idris al-Fasi 1760–1837 Major reformer Ahmad al-Alawi 1869–1934 Alawiyya branch Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari b. 1974 Present guide"},{"url":"/history/tariqa-origins","title":"Origins of the Tariqa Karkariya","section":"What makes it distinct","sectionId":"what-makes-it-distinct","content":"Within the broad Shadhili family, the Karkariya is characterized by: **Centrality of the Ism al-Mufrad** — inherited directly from the Alawiyya **The seven-pillar framework** — a clear pedagogical structure for the path **Openness to global murids** — active outreach to Western seekers **Contemporary language** — the Shaykh translates classical teaching into modern terms without diluting it"},{"url":"/history/tariqa-origins","title":"Origins of the Tariqa Karkariya","section":"Fes as spiritual home","sectionId":"fes-as-spiritual-home","content":"The Tariqa is centered in **Fes**, Morocco — a city with an unbroken tradition of Islamic scholarship and Sufi life since the ninth century. The city is home to the Qarawiyyin mosque-university (founded 859 CE), numerous zawiyas, and the shrines of great Moroccan saints."},{"url":"/history/tariqa-origins","title":"Origins of the Tariqa Karkariya","section":"See also","sectionId":"see-also","content":"Silsila Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari Ahmad al-Alawi"},{"url":"/podcasts/episode-001-what-is-the-tariqa","title":"Episode 1: What is the Tariqa Karkariya?","content":"An introduction to the Tariqa — its origins, method, and the question of what a Sufi order is in the modern world."},{"url":"/podcasts/episode-001-what-is-the-tariqa","title":"Episode 1: What is the Tariqa Karkariya?","section":"Episode details","sectionId":"episode-details","content":"**Guest:** Murid speaking on behalf of the community (anonymized)\n&#x2A;*Length:** \\~45 minutes\n&#x2A;*Topics:** Introduction to the Tariqa, the Silsila, the role of the Shaykh Mohamed Faouzi al-Karkari"},{"url":"/podcasts/episode-001-what-is-the-tariqa","title":"Episode 1: What is the Tariqa Karkariya?","section":"Transcript excerpt","sectionId":"transcript-excerpt","content":"**Host:** We're starting from the beginning today. For someone who has never heard of the Tariqa Karkariya, what would you say it is? **Guest:** It's a Sufi path — a school of the heart. The word \"tariqa\" itself means a way or a road. It's a school for traveling toward God, with a living guide who has himself traveled the road and can help you navigate it. **Host:** Why do you need a guide? Can't you just read books? **Guest:** You can read all the maps in the world and still get lost. The guide"},{"url":"/podcasts/episode-002-the-seven-pillars","title":"Episode 2: The Seven Pillars Explained","content":"A deep-dive conversation on the seven foundational practices of the Tariqa Karkariya."},{"url":"/podcasts/episode-002-the-seven-pillars","title":"Episode 2: The Seven Pillars Explained","section":"Episode details","sectionId":"episode-details","content":"**Length:** \\~60 minutes\n&#x2A;*Topics:** All seven pillars — Wird, Hadra, Muraqqa', Ism al-Mufrad, Siyaha, Khalwa, Sirr"},{"url":"/podcasts/episode-002-the-seven-pillars","title":"Episode 2: The Seven Pillars Explained","section":"Transcript excerpt","sectionId":"transcript-excerpt","content":"**Host:** Today we're going through the seven pillars. Let's start with the wird. What is it, and why is it the foundation? **Guest:** The wird is the daily litany — a specific sequence of remembrance formulas that you recite morning and evening. It's the foundation because it's what keeps the connection alive day by day. The Shaykh describes it as the rope between the murid and his Lord. **Host:** It sounds simple. **Guest:** It is simple. That's the point. Consistency beats complexity every ti"},{"url":"/podcasts/episode-003-on-the-shaykh","title":"Episode 3: The Role of the Shaykh","content":"What does it mean to have a living spiritual guide? A candid discussion on the Shaykh-murid relationship."},{"url":"/podcasts/episode-003-on-the-shaykh","title":"Episode 3: The Role of the Shaykh","section":"Episode details","sectionId":"episode-details","content":"**Length:** \\~50 minutes\n&#x2A;*Topics:** The role of the Shaykh, the Bay'a, trust and obedience, common misconceptions"},{"url":"/podcasts/episode-003-on-the-shaykh","title":"Episode 3: The Role of the Shaykh","section":"Transcript excerpt","sectionId":"transcript-excerpt","content":"**Host:** The concept of a spiritual guide — a Shaykh — is strange to many people raised in the modern West. What is the Shaykh actually doing? **Guest:** He's a spiritual physician. The Quran and Sunna are the medicine, and the Shaykh is the one who knows how to administer it. Anyone can read a medical textbook. That doesn't make them a doctor. The Shaykh has been trained — through the Silsila, through years of practice, through his own transformation — to see the diseases of the soul and presc"},{"url":"/podcasts","title":"Podcasts","content":"Published AFFiNE pages in Podcasts."},{"url":"/podcasts","title":"Podcasts","section":"Podcasts","sectionId":"podcasts","content":"Episode 1: What is the Tariqa Karkariya? Episode 2: The Seven Pillars Explained Episode 3: The Role of the Shaykh"},{"url":"/tags/foundation","title":"Foundation","content":"Core foundational concepts of the Tariqa Karkariya. Notes covering the foundations of the path — the pillars, the covenant, and the core concepts a murid encounters first."},{"url":"/tags","title":"Tags","content":"Published AFFiNE pages in Tags."},{"url":"/tags","title":"Tags","section":"Tags","sectionId":"tags","content":"Foundation Practice"},{"url":"/tags/practice","title